  "text": "To solve this problem, we need to understand how the arcs form a hexagon. The hexagon is formed by connecting three congruent circular arcs with radii of 7, 13, and 15 units, respectively. The arcs intersect at their endpoints, and the hexagon is a single, closed curve.\n\nGiven that the length of the shortest side of the hexagon is 25 units, we know that the shortest side is formed by the arcs with radii of 7 units. This implies that the shortest side is the distance between the points where the arcs with radii of 7 units intersect, which are the endpoints of the arc.\n\nThe shortest side of the hexagon is thus the arc length of the arc with radius 7 units. However, since the arcs intersect at their endpoints, we need to consider the common chord formed by the intersection of the two arcs with radii 7 and 13 units. This common chord is the shortest side of the hexagon.\n\nThe length of the shortest side can be calculated using the formula for the length of a segment of a circle. The length of a segment of a circle is given by:\n\n\\[ L = r \\cdot \\theta \\]\n\nwhere \\( r \\) is the radius of the circle and \\( \\theta \\) is the central angle in radians. However, since the arcs intersect at their endpoints, we need to consider the common chord, which forms a right triangle with the radii and the chord length.\n\nThe chord length can be found using the formula:\n\n\\[ \\text{chord length} = 2r \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\theta}{2}\\right) \\]\n\nGiven that the chord length is 25 units and the radius is 7 units, we have:\n\n\\[ 25 = 2 \\cdot 7 \\cdot \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\theta}{2}\\right) \\]\n\n\\[ 25 = 14 \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\theta}{2}\\right) \\]\n\n\\[ \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\theta}{2}\\right) = \\frac{25}{14} \\]\n\nSince \\( \\sin\\left(\\frac{\\theta}{2}\\right) \\) cannot be greater than 1, we have a contradiction. This implies that our initial assumption about the length of the shortest side is incorrect.\n\nGiven that the shortest side is 25 units, we need to reconsider the geometry. The correct approach is to use the formula for the length of a segment of a circle, but we need to use the radius and the central angle in radians.\n\nGiven that the length of the shortest side is 25 units, we have:\n\n\\[ 25 = r \\cdot \\theta \\]\n\nSince the radius is 7 units, we have:\n\n\\[ 25 = 7 \\cdot \\theta \\]\n\n\\[ \\theta = \\frac{25}{7} \\]\n\nThe area enclosed by the hexagon can be calculated using the formula for the area of a segment of a circle. The segment area is given by:\n\n\\[ A = \\frac{1}{2} r^2 (\\theta - \\sin \\theta) \\]\n\nSubstituting \\( r = 7 \\) and \\( \\theta = \\frac{25}{7} \\), we have:\n\n\\[ A = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 7^2 \\left( \\frac{25}{7} - \\sin \\left( \\frac{25}{7} \\right) \\right) \\]\n\n\\[ A = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 49 \\left( \\frac{25}{7} - \\sin \\left( \\frac{25}{7} \\right) \\right) \\]\n\n\\[ A = 24.5 \\left( \\frac{25}{7} - \\sin \\left( \\frac{25}{7} \\right) \\right) \\]\n\nUsing the approximate value of \\( \\sin \\left( \\frac{25}{7} \\right) \\), which is approximately 0.17, we have:\n\n\\[ A = 24.5 \\left( \\frac{25}{7} - 0.17 \\right) \\]\n\n\\[ A = 24.5 \\left( 3.57 - 0.17 \\right) \\]\n\n\\[ A = 24.5 \\cdot 3.4 \\]\n\n\\[ A = 83.3 \\]\n\nThe area enclosed by the hexagon is approximately \\( \\boxed{83.3} \\).",
